# Line endings, settled once so they cannot drift per machine.
#
# Windows sets core.autocrlf=true by default, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. For source files that is harmless. For the git hooks it is not: a
# shebang line ending in "\r" makes the kernel look for an interpreter literally
# called "/bin/sh\r", and the hook dies with "bad interpreter". The guards would
# stop running for the next person who clones, silently — which is the worst way
# for a safety net to fail.

# Normalise everything to LF in the repository, and check out LF everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the executable scripts.
*.sh          text eol=lf
.githooks/*   text eol=lf

# Binaries must be stored byte-for-byte. Without this, "text=auto" can mangle a
# DOCX or PDF that happens to look textual to git's heuristics — and this repo
# commits the generated sample offer document as evidence.
*.pdf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary

# Keep generated artefacts and the vendored corpus provenance out of diffs and
# out of GitHub's language statistics, so the repository is reported as the
# TypeScript project it is.
package-lock.json          linguist-generated=true
backtest_output/**         linguist-generated=true
legacy_streamlit/**        linguist-vendored=true
